.TH OB-SESSION-RECORDER 8 "September 2026" "open-bastion" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
ob-session-recorder \- record SSH sessions on an Open Bastion host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ob-session-recorder
.RB [ \-c
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR FORMAT ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR DIR ]
.br
.B ob-session-recorder
.BR \-h | \-v
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ob-session-recorder
is run by
.BR sshd (8)
as
.B ForceCommand
for every recorded session; it is not meant to be run by hand. It runs as the
recorded user. The recording itself is written by
.BR ob-record-sink (8),
a root service the user cannot reach, so the user can neither read, alter nor
delete it.
.PP
For an interactive session, or a command
.RB ( SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND ),
it starts
.BR ob-record-connect (1),
which connects to the sink and sends a one-line JSON header, then runs the
user's shell or command under
.BR script (1),
whose typescript goes through a FIFO to the connector and on to the sink.
If the sink cannot be reached, the session is refused: recording is
mandatory.
.PP
A genuine file transfer cannot run under a terminal \(em its binary protocol
would be corrupted \(em so it runs with raw input and output, and the sink
records its metadata only (format
.BR transfer ).
Because that means not recording the session's stream, a command is only
treated as a transfer when it is exactly what a genuine client sends:
.TP
.B rsync \-\-server \fR[\fB\-\-sender\fR] \fIFLAGS\fR [\fI\-\-long\-option\fR[\fB=\fIvalue\fR]...] \fB. \fIPATH\fR...
with the option list of
.BR rrsync (1),
minus
.B \-\-daemon
and
.B \-s
.RB ( \-\-secluded\-args ).
.TP
.B scp \fR[\fB\-v\fR] [\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-p\fR] [\fB\-d\fR] \fB\-t\fR|\fB\-f\fR [\fB\-\-\fR] \fIPATH\fR...
the legacy protocol
.RB ( "scp \-O" ),
one word per flag.
.TP
.B internal\-sftp\fR | \fIsftp\-server path\fR [\fIoptions\fR]
the sftp subsystem, which sshd passes as
.B SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND
under
.BR ForceCommand ;
modern
.BR scp (1)
uses it too.
.B internal\-sftp
runs the system
.B sftp\-server
binary.
.PP
A receiving
.B rsync
or
.B scp \-t
takes one path, a sender one or more. Paths may use backslash escapes, a
leading
.B ~
and wildcards, which the recorder expands itself (pathname expansion only).
Any unescaped shell metacharacter or control character, anything appended to
these forms, or a session that requested a terminal, and the command is
recorded as an ordinary session, with a warning in the log. A transfer is run
as an argument vector, never through a shell, and the program is taken from a
fixed root-owned path, never from
.BR PATH .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " FILE" "\fR, \fP\-\-config " FILE
Read the configuration from
.I FILE
instead of
.IR /etc/open-bastion/session-recorder.conf .
.TP
.BI \-f " FORMAT" "\fR, \fP\-\-format " FORMAT
Recording format. Only
.B script
is supported;
.B asciinema
and
.B ttyrec
fall back to it.
.TP
.BI \-d " DIR" "\fR, \fP\-\-dir " DIR
Ignored. The storage path belongs to
.BR ob-record-sink (8).
Accepted for compatibility.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.I /etc/open-bastion/session-recorder.conf
is read only if it is owned by root and not writable by group or others.
.TP
.B format
As
.BR \-f .
.TP
.B max_duration
Maximum session duration in seconds (default 86400;
.BR ob-bastion-setup (8)
writes 28800).
.B 0
disables it. A value that is not a number of seconds falls back to the
default, with an error in the log. At the limit the session is hung up, as a
client disconnect would, and whatever is left is killed 5 seconds later. The
recording is delivered in full and ends
.BR completed ;
the timeout is logged.
.TP
.B sessions_dir
Ignored (see
.BR \-d ).
.PP
.BR recording_compress_after_days " and " recording_retention_days
belong to
.BR ob-session-prune (8).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The recorder reads what sshd sets for the session:
.BR SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND ,
.B SSH_CLIENT
(client address, informational),
.B SSH_TTY
(whether a terminal was requested),
.BR HOME " and " SHELL .
.PP
Nothing in the environment changes how, or whether, the session is recorded.
.BR OB_RECORDER_CONFIG ,
.BR OB_RECORDER_FORMAT ,
.B OB_MAX_SESSION
and
.B OB_SESSIONS_DIR
are ignored since 0.7.0; so is
.BR OB_RECORD_SOCKET ,
which the recorder sets itself for the connector. The script runs under
.B bash \-p
(no
.BR BASH_ENV ,
no functions or shell options imported from the environment), sets
.B PATH
to the system directories, and takes
.B ob-record-connect
only from a root-owned
.I /usr/bin
or
.IR /usr/local/bin .
The username comes from the real uid, never from
.BR USER .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/open-bastion/session-recorder.conf
Configuration.
.TP
.I /run/open-bastion/rec.sock
The sink's socket.
.TP
.I /var/lib/open-bastion/sessions/USER/TIMESTAMP_ID.typescript
The recording, written by
.BR ob-record-sink (8),
root-owned.
.TP
.I /var/lib/open-bastion/sessions/USER/TIMESTAMP_ID.json
Its metadata, including the sink-observed status:
.BR completed ,
.B truncated
(byte or duration cap) or
.B aborted
(the stream stopped without its end-of-stream marker).
.TP
.I /tmp/ob-rec.XXXXXXXXXX/stream
The FIFO between
.B script
and the connector, in a private directory removed when the session ends.
.SH LIMITS
The recorder, the connector and
.B script
run as the recorded user, who can kill them. Killing the connector is
detected (the recording ends
.BR aborted ,
and
.B script
dies at its next output); killing the
.B max_duration
watchdog is not prevented. The bound the user cannot lift is the sink's own
duration cap (see
.BR ob-record-sink (8)).
A process detached from the session with
.B setsid
escapes the terminal recording; see the session containment settings of
.BR ob-bastion-setup (8).
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status of the shell, command or transfer; 1 when the session is
refused because it cannot be recorded; 129 when the session was hung up
(client gone, or
.BR max_duration ).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ob-record-sink (8),
.BR ob-record-connect (1),
.BR ob-session-prune (8),
.BR ob-bastion-setup (8),
.BR script (1),
.BR rrsync (1),
.BR sshd_config (5)
